ials so we use silicon dioxide  because it's easy to generate and also  easy to measure nanomaterials sometimes  behave very uniquely as we discussed  they have different physical  characteristics that make them hard to  contain hard to analyze and so it can be  difficult to adapt this test to every  type of material material you may be  using kind of coverage that's already  hard to know how dangerous stuff is  without more data and also unlike the  drug industry nanomaterials typically  don't have the burden of health and  safety when they're generated so  obviously FDA in the United States  regulates the toxicity and information  that on how a drug operates but if  you're manufacturing a component for a  computer and there is a generation of a  nano particulate you may not have the  same burden of safety to a governmental  organization like you may have with a  drug submitted through the FDA and also  to that and there's not really a good  regulatory guidance on how to behave  with nanomaterials there are information  publications out there NIOSH has nano  materials handling guidelines but  certainly  could always be more information out  there so with that the best thing you  can look at is containing those two  those procedures as best as you can  until further information is made  available with that I'm going to open it  up to some questions I hope you all  enjoyed the presentation and certainly  ask that you ask any questions now  during this live period and if you have  other questions after we're done you can  also send them to us via email and we'll  answer them individually  thank you David for your informative  presentation we will now start the live  Q&amp;A portion of the webinar if you have a  question you'd like to ask please do so  now just click on the answer question  box located on the far left your screen  and we'll answer as many of your  questions as we have time for so let's  get started our first question is we're  currently using a fume hood for a  process that generates nanoparticle  particulate is there a way to make our  fume hood suitable for nanoparticle  containment  so that's a great question the you know  obviously every lab has a lot of stuff  in it already I don't think I've seen a  lab recently that has a lot of extra  space on hand so if your only option for  any kind of containment versus open bent  as a fume hood the first thing you want  to do is assess if you can make such a  modification and one one consideration  that you might be able to adapt is to  add a filter in the ductwork there are  some manufacturers out there who me who  provide in ductwork hepa and ulpa  filters and options but I would caution  against using this as a primary fix for  your fume hood because you have to also  consider that any distance between where  you're generating particulates and where  that filter is located is now going to  be a contaminated space so imagine for a  second that you're generating  particulates and the filter is located  somewhere a hundred feet into the duct  run anytime that filter is modified or  adjusted or cut or or changed in the  future it's got to be treated as if it's  contaminated with nanomaterials so it's  especially important to see if you can  contrack particulates on a filter as  close to the source where they're  generated as possible  now David our next question are these  containment measures specific two types  of nanoparticles or are they for all  types of nanoparticles  um so they are generally specific are  I'm sorry they're generally applicable  to all nano containment nano  particulates although you know there are  some that be a very uniquely some that  are also air sensitive so there are  alternate containment options there are  glove boxes that can be completely  flushed of all oxygen and moisture and  still contain so I I would say if you're  handling a relatively inert simplistic  nano material carbon nanotubes etc those  are good fits for her for hoods with  airflow but if you have something very  unique has additional dangers I'd say  it's best to talk with your safety staff  first and and then get in touch with  someone like ourselves to discuss  potentials for containment  do all nano hoods need to be externally  vented  um I would say generally most are not  actually but the driving force there is  really one if you have chemical vapors  or things that would not be trapped by  the hepa or ALPA filter that would be  items that are in addition to the nano  particulates that you're using that  would be case number one where you would  not where you might consider an exhaust  for a hood the other one is perhaps your  safety personnel on site want some  additional protection so let's say you  know you moved the hood or damaged the  HEPA filter and then certaintly  and had not had it retested yet if the  hood was used and then exhausted your  could potentially plays an induct HEPA  filtration or LP filtration to act as a  redundant filter  now are there any drawbacks to using an  opus using opus l turfs  of all the filters so they're they're  really similar to a HEPA filter they're  just more dense material and in doing  that you're basically you're providing  an additional level of protection but  they also require more airflow a force  to go through that filter so if you have  a very messy application with lots of  powder just be prepared to change that  filter more frequently another thing  about that is if you have nanomaterials  that are in a on the larger side of  things closer to the point three micron  range you might consider using a HEPA  filter as that the HEPA filter will  still provide protection for those  larger particles at a highly efficient  rate and may afford you some longer  filter life  now David our next our next question is  going to be has does have two parts so  how about the bio polymeric  nanoparticles and can they be trapped by  these containment methods  good question and actually if you don't  mind I'm going to retain that question  to be answered by Matt hull Matt wasn't  able to join us today but is a nano  containment expert and if that's okay  I'll just defer that question to him and  have him follow up via email  so let's continue on let's continue on  with our next one now is there a good  way to measure for nanoparticles in our  lab environment yeah so so you can get a  particle counter specific for nano  material size constituents I discussed  one option there are lots and lots of  manufacturers out there who may make  particle counters for the nano scale  items and I would say if you don't have  one in your laboratory right now and  you're doing a lot of nano work you  should contact some a safety person on  site and consider maybe doing an  assessment either with an outside party  on a case-by-case basis or perhaps just  purchasing one of those yourself for for  as much testing as you need to control  and carry out on-site they do they're  not exactly cheap though I will caution  that so it's an investment to consider  versus having a third party measure and  provide assessments for yourself  and it looks like that is all the time  that we have for today so thank you  David do you have any final comments for  our audience no I want to thank  everybody for joining us today and I  know there are a couple questions that  we'll need to answer from Matt hull I  seriously advise all you to to go and  check out the nano safe website he's  offering a very nice approach to  consider measuring and doing assessments  for nano containment and if you have  questions about procedures in your  laboratory or working environment right  now he would certainly be one of the  foremost experts to contact and discuss  those applications with thank you again  David for your time today Andrew  important research we would also like to  thank laberd's and our sponsor lab Khan  Co for underwriting today's educational  webcast before we go I'd like to thank  the audi